    "body": "We first learned about the `eval` issues sometime back when we first started adding multiline init scripts to starship. If you look back far enough in the commit history, you'll find all sorts of weird semicolon/newlines/whitespace shenanigans in `init.rs`---those were all just put in to deal with the quirks of `eval`.\r\n\r\nIf you look at what the result of `echo (starship init fish)`, you should see that it's just `source (starship init fish --print-full-init | psub)`, which is the command that the shell will `eval` in order to actually do the initialization. This isn't a perfect system (#257 found that if you alias `cat`, `psub` will break), but it was the solution that we came up with at the time to fit two goals:\r\n\r\n  1) Allow multiline init scripts without requiring semicolon sorcery\r\n  2) Preserve the old init commands for all shells, i.e. `eval (something)`\r\n\r\nI think as we move the starship towards 1.0 and the initscripts get more and more complicated, we might want to consider just dropping this altogether and providing standalone shell adapters like denysdovhan's [robbyrussell-node](https://github.com/denysdovhan/robbyrussell-node) project.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
